  <page>
    <title>Stonewall Jackson (musician)</title>
    <id>185085</id>
    <revision>
      <id>41127112</id>
      <timestamp>2006-02-25T05:33:02Z</timestamp>
      <contributor>
        <username>Deborah-jl</username>
        <id>89168</id>
      </contributor>
      <minor />
      <comment>fix disambig</comment>
      <text xml:space="preserve">'''Stonewall Jackson''' (born [[November 6]], [[1932]]) was a [[Country_music|Country]] musician. During Country's Golden Honky Tonk Era in the late 50's and early 60's. 

Stonewall Jackson was one of the most popular country stars of the late 50's the early [[1960s]]. He was named after the famous general [[Stonewall Jackson|Thomas &quot;Stonewall&quot; Jackson]], who was an ancestor.

His most popular song was &quot;Waterloo&quot; in [[1959]].  The song is a haunting but catchy tune which states that &quot;Everybody has to meet his [[Battle of Waterloo|Waterloo]]&quot;, meaning their [[fate]] or comeuppance.  The song cites [[Adam and Eve|Adam]], [[Napoleon]] and [[Tom Dooley]] as examples.

Stonewall Jackson was the first to join the Grand Old Opry before he got a recording contract. He signed to Columbia Records and debuted in 1958 with &quot;Don't Be Angry&quot;. The song didn't score in the Top 40 (country), but it got him recognition. His break thru record debuted in the Country Top 40 in late 1958 with a song written by a sophomoric George Jones, &quot;Life to Go&quot;. It got him enough recognition that it peaked at #2 in early 1959. His follow up record, the above mentioned, &quot;Waterloo&quot; , scored at #1 for 5 weeks, it even crossed over into Top 40 Pop charts and peaked at #4. His next #1 came in 1964, with &quot;B.J. The D.J.&quot; (about an over-worked country music station DJ, who crashes his car in a rain storm because of bad tires). He was the first to record a live record from the Grand Old Opry, in 1963 with &quot;Old Showboat&quot;. Other classics include, &quot;the Carpet On The Floor&quot;, &quot;Why I'm Walkin'&quot;,&quot;A Wound Time Can't Erase&quot;, &quot;I Washed My Hands in Muddy Water&quot;. From 1958 to 1971, he had 35 Top 40 Country Hits.  Along with Ray Price, Stonewall Jackson is concidered a cornerstone, after Hank Williams &amp; Lefty Frizzell, in the hard drivin' Honky Tonk&quot; sound in the late 50's and early 60's. It could be said that Stonewall was one of the last great honky tonkers, before the Nashville Sound and later the Bakersfield Sound took over Country Music in the 60's, before The Beatles and the British Invasion.
{{Musician-stub}}


[[Category:1932 births|Jackson, Stonewall]]
[[Category:Living people|Jackson, Stonewall]]
[[Category:Country singers|Jackson, Stonewall]]</text>
    </revision>
  </page>
