  <page>
    <title>Andrew Wells</title>
    <id>185089</id>
    <revision>
      <id>41693023</id>
      <timestamp>2006-03-01T02:33:08Z</timestamp>
      <contributor>
        <username>Gonzalo84</username>
        <id>536794</id>
      </contributor>
      <comment>/* Trivia */</comment>
      <text xml:space="preserve">{{Infobox Buffyverse Character|
 Image=[[Image:AndrewWells01.jpg|260px]]&lt;br&gt;[[Tom Lenk]] as '''Andrew Wells'''|
 Title=Andrew Wells|
 First=[[Flooded (Buffy episode)|Flooded]] (Buffy)|
 Last=[[The Girl in Question (Angel episode)|The Girl in Question]] (Angel)|
 Creator=Joss Whedon|
 Name=Andrew Wells|
 Status=Alive|
 Kind=Human|
 Affiliation=Disciple of [[Rupert Giles|Giles]], [[Watcher's Council]], ally of the [[Scooby Gang]], former pawn of [[The First Evil]], partner of [[Jonathan Levinson|Jonathan]], member of the Trio and [[Sunnydale High]] student.|
 Powers=&lt;br&gt;
* Skilled demon summoner, adept at employing pipes, flutes and other wind instruments for this.
* Watcher training grants him moderate fighting techniques and knowledge in demonology and the dark arts.|
 Actor=[[Tom Lenk]]|
}}

'''Andrew Wells''' is a [[fictional character]] in the [[United States|U.S.]] [[television program|television series]] ''[[Buffy the Vampire Slayer]]'', played by [[Thomas Lenk|Tom Lenk]]. He was the younger brother of a character seen in an earlier episode, [[Tucker Wells]], who summoned [[Hellhound (Buffyverse)|hellhounds]] to attack the Prom in season three (''[[The Prom (Buffy episode)|The Prom]]''). He himself has skills in demon-raising and animal control, although these were inexplicably never used after season six; he credits his brother with giving him the courage to develop this talent. It is perhaps not coincidental that a demon-summoning family living atop a [[Hellmouth]] would be named &quot;Wells,&quot; when &quot;well&quot; is, after all, just another word for a hole in the ground.

At some point during Buffy's senior year, Andrew summoned Winged Monkey Demons to attack the school's production of ''[[Romeo and Juliet]]'', apparently for no other reason than to amuse himself. Although the [[Scooby Gang]] no doubt investigated at the time, they did not discover that Andrew was responsible, and a few years later they had forgotten the incident, simply one of any number of minor bits of magical mayhem that plagued Sunnydale. Years after the fact, both Jonathan and Warren recalled the incident with amusement; since Warren, Buffy's contemporary, is known to have spent only his senior year at Sunnydale High (He attended the prom to which Tucker sent the hellhounds.), his memory of the occasion proves that it occurred during his senior year, which was also Buffy's.

{{spoiler}}

Although presumably attending [[Sunnydale High]] along with [[Buffy Summers|Buffy]] (most likely a grade or two below her), Andrew was only introduced (long after the destruction of the High School) in season six, as a member of the Trio, Buffy's self-styled &quot;arch nemesises&quot; [sic]. Indeed, Andrew was arguably the most important member of the group, since many of their endeavors relied upon his ability to summon and control various demons.

[[Warren Mears]], the leader of the gang, was almost entirely driven by greed and lust for power; whereas [[Jonathan Levinson]] apparently joined the gang for excitement, was repelled by Warren's ruthlessness and eventually helped save Buffy's life. Andrew occupied a sort of middle ground between these two, initially, as before, simply seeking power and amusement, but becoming more evil as the show progressed, for example, cheering &quot;kill her!&quot; as Warren fought Buffy, and actually killing Jonathan in season seven. After this Andrew became somewhat remorseful and repentant, culminating in the episode ''[[Storyteller (Buffy episode)|Storyteller]]''. In this episode Buffy forces him to face up to the seriousness of his actions. Afterwards Andrew joined Buffy as a comic sidekick in the fight against the First Evil. Thus, the character of Andrew is an example of the show's strong emphasis on [[redemption]]. In two episodes of ''[[Angel (TV series)|Angel]]'''s fifth season, Andrew returns, and the character appears to have matured slightly.  He is now under the mentorship of [[Rupert Giles]], who is training him as a Watcher, presumably alongside many others, as now hundreds of Slayers require guidance. Spike seems to have an amused if exasperated affection for him.

A running joke in the show is that for a while nobody knew who Andrew was, and he was often referred to as &quot;the other guy&quot;, &quot;Tucker's brother&quot;, and so on. This is a reference to the fact that the character of Andrew was created at a last minute by the show's writers, as a replacement for the Tucker Wells character. Originally, the writers wanted Brad Kane to return to the show as his season three character Tucker Wells, who would be the leader of the &quot;Trio&quot; with Jonathan and Warren as his flunkies. But the two sides failed to come to an agreement regarding a contract, resulting in Lenk being cast as Tucker's younger brother.

==Homosexuality==
Other points of interest in Andrew's character include his apparent [[The Closet|closeted]] [[homosexuality]]. Andrew is portrayed as having a crush on his friend and leader Warren, to the extent that the First Evil takes on Warren's appearance to seduce Andrew into killing their teammate Jonathan as part of its scheme. When it becomes clear that the First is using him, Andrew begins a friendship with the vampire [[Spike (Buffy the Vampire Slayer)|Spike]]. Even though Spike nearly kills Andrew while under the influence of the First Evil, the two form a close bond, with Andrew alternating between hero worship towards Spike and being overly affectionate with Spike, who is either oblivious to Andrew's possible homosexuality or, more likely, simply doesn't care enough about Andrew to notice.

Andrew's sexuality is further complicated in his final appearance (''[[The Girl in Question (Angel episode)|The Girl in Question]]'').  Now surprisingly mature, Andrew lectures both Spike and [[Angel (vampire)|Angel]] about dating and women. As the scene ends, Andrew is shown leaving for the opera in [[James Bond]]-style attire with two attractive women, referring to them as his dates.

Many fans commented that Andrew's dates do not outright mean that the character has suddenly become heterosexual (which is the popular interpretation of said scene).  Accompanying two women instead of one, Andrew can be seen as a mere escort for the two women; some take the date as a symptom of overcompensation for Andrew's still-repressed homosexuality. They cite Andrew's overly affectionate interaction with Spike earlier in the episode as a sign that Andrew is still gay and that his dates are not a sign that he has converted fulltime to heterosexuality.  He could also, of course, have always been bisexual.


==Trivia==

- Andrew was created when Brad Kane was unavailable to return to the role of Andrew's brother Tucker. The Trio was originally supposed to be formed by Warren, Jonathan and Tucker. The fact that Tucker, being a character that only appeared once in the Buffyverse, was remembered, unlike Andrew, was a running gag during season's six and seven. Andrew is referred to characters that don't know or remember who he is as &quot;Tucker's brother&quot;.

==Appearances==
Andrew has appeared in:

; ''[[Buffy the Vampire Slayer]]'' : Andrew appeared as a guest in 26 episodes:
: Season 6 (2001, 2002) - ''[[Flooded (Buffy episode)|Flooded]]''; ''[[Life Serial (Buffy episode)|Life Serial]]''; ''[[Smashed (Buffy episode)|Smashed]]''; ''[[Gone (Buffy episode)|Gone]]''; ''[[Dead Things (Buffy episode)|Dead Things]]''; ''[[Normal Again (Buffy episode)|Normal Again]]''; ''[[Entropy (Buffy episode)|Entropy]]''; ''[[Seeing Red (Buffy episode)|Seeing Red]]''; ''[[Villains (Buffy episode)|Villains]]''; ''[[Two to Go (Buffy episode)|Two to Go]]''; ''[[Grave (Buffy episode)|Grave]]''.
: Season 7 (2002, 2003) - ''[[Conversations with Dead People (Buffy episode)|Conversations with Dead People]]''; ''[[Never Leave Me (Buffy episode)|Never Leave Me]]''; ''[[Bring on the Night (Buffy episode)|Bring on the Night]]''; ''[[Showtime (Buffy episode)|Showtime]]''; ''[[Potential (Buffy episode)|Potential]]''; ''[[The Killer in Me (Buffy episode)|The Killer in Me]]''; ''[[First Date (Buffy episode)|First Date]]''; ''[[Get It Done (Buffy episode)|Get It Done]]''; ''[[Storyteller (Buffy episode)|Storyteller]]''; ''[[Lies My Parents Told Me (Buffy episode)|Lies My Parents Told Me]]''; ''[[Dirty Girls (Buffy episode)|Dirty Girls]]''; ''[[Empty Places (Buffy episode)|Empty Places]]''; ''[[End of Days (Buffy episode)|End of Days]]''; ''[[Chosen (Buffy episode)|Chosen]]

; ''[[Angel (TV series)|Angel]]'' : Andrew appeared as a guest in two episodes:
: Season 5 (2003,2004) - ''[[Damage (Angel episode)|Damage]]''; ''[[The Girl in Question (Angel episode)|The Girl in Question]]

[[Category:Buffy the Vampire Slayer characters|Wells, Andrew]]
[[Category:Buffy the Vampire Slayer villains|Wells, Andrew]]
[[Category:Fictional LGBT characters|Wells, Andrew]]</text>
