  <page>
    <title>Chechen language</title>
    <id>185118</id>
    <revision>
      <id>38420412</id>
      <timestamp>2006-02-06T05:05:22Z</timestamp>
      <contributor>
        <ip>154.35.1.8</ip>
      </contributor>
      <comment>/* Vocabulary */ added a missing semicolon</comment>
      <text xml:space="preserve">{{language
|name=Chechen
|nativename=Нохчийн мотт, Noxçiyn mott
|states=[[Russia]]
|region=autonomous republic of [[Chechnya]]
|speakers=944,600
|familycolor=Caucasian
|fam1=[[Languages of the Caucasus|Caucasian]] ''(geographical convention)''
|fam2=[[North Caucasian languages|North]] ''(disputed)''
|fam3=[[Northeast Caucasian languages|Northeast]]
|fam4=[[Nakh languages|Nakh]]
|fam4=Veinakh (Chechen-Ingush)
|nation=[[Chechnya]] (autonomous republic of [[Russia]])
|iso1=ce|iso2=che|iso3=che}}

The '''Chechen language''' has about 1,200,000 speakers, most of whom live in Russia. 

==Classification==
The Chechen language is one of the [[languages of the Caucasus]]. Linguistically, it is a [[Northeast Caucasian languages|Northeast Caucasian language]] with partial mutual intelligibility with the [[Ingush language]] and intermediary dialects.

Languages indigenous to the Caucasus are not members of any language families spoken elsewhere in the world.

==Geographic distribution==
Chechen is spoken by about 950,000, in Chechnya and (due to the [[Chechen diaspora]]) [[Middle East]] countries, especially [[Jordan]].

===Official status===
Chechen is an official language of [[Chechnya]], an [[autonomous republic]] of [[Russia]].

===Dialects===
There are a number of Chechen dialects:
*[[Ploskost]]
*[[Itumkala (Shatoi)]]
*[[Melkhin]]
*[[Kistin]]
*[[Cheberloi]]
*[[Akkin (Aux)]]

==Sounds==
Some characteristics of Chechen include its wealth of consonants and sounds similar to [[Arabic language|Arabic]] or [[Native American languages]], a large vowel system resembling [[Swedish language|Swedish]] or [[German language|German]], several [[grammatical genders]], and a complex [[phrase structure]].

The Chechen language has (like most indigenous languages of the Caucasus) a large number of consonants: about 31 (depending on the dialect and the analysis), more than for most languages of Europe. Unlike most other languages of the Caucasus, it also has an extensive inventory of vowels and diphthongs: about 27 (depending on dialect and analysis), similar in number and phonetics to the vowel systems of the [[Scandinavian languages]], German, and [[Finnish language|Finnish]]. None of the spelling systems used for Chechen so far have distinguished the vowels with complete accuracy.

==Grammar==
Chechen also presents interesting challenges for lexicography, as creating new words in the language relies on fixation of whole phrases rather than adding to the end of existing words or combining existing words. It can be difficult to decide which phrases belong in the dictionary.

==Vocabulary==
Native Chechen words are few in number (not more than 3000). There are many borrowings from [[Russian language|Russian]], Turkic languages (mostly from [[Kumyk language|Kumyk]]), [[Arabic language|Arabic]], and some &amp;mdash; from [[Persian language|Persian]], [[Alans|Alanian]] ([[Ossetic language|Ossetic]]), and [[Georgian language|Georgian]]. Chechen and [[Ingush]] scholars have found links to the ancient [[Cuneiform script|cuneiform]] languages [[Hurrian language|Hurrian]] and [[Urartian language|Urartian]].

==History==
The Chechen literary language was created after the [[October Revolution]], and the [[Latin alphabet]] began to be used instead of Arabic for Chechen writing in the mid-[[1920s]]. In [[1938]], the [[Cyrillic alphabet]] was adopted. With the declaration of the Chechen republic in [[1992]], some Chechen speakers returned to the Latin alphabet. The Chechen diaspora in [[Jordan]], [[Turkey]] and [[Syria]] is fluent but generally not literate in Chechen except for individuals who have made efforts to learn the writing system, and of course the Cyrillic alphabet is not generally known in these countries.

==External links==
{{InterWiki|code=ce}}
*[http://www.ethnologue.com/show_language.asp?code=che Ethnologue report for Chechen]
*[http://www.rosettaproject.org/live/search/detailedlanguagerecord?ethnocode=CJC  Rosetta Project entry for Chechen (includes word list)]
*[http://ingush.narod.ru/chech/book/ ''Indigenous Language of the Caucasus (Chechen)'', grammatical sketch of Chechen language]
*[http://www.evertype.com/alphabets/chechen.pdf The Cyrillic and Latin Chechen alphabets]
[[Category:Caucasian languages]]
[[Category:Chechnya]]
[[Category:Languages of Russia]]

