  <page>
    <title>Thermal radiation</title>
    <id>185239</id>
    <revision>
      <id>41986418</id>
      <timestamp>2006-03-03T01:31:53Z</timestamp>
      <contributor>
        <username>Daniel11</username>
        <id>63985</id>
      </contributor>
      <minor />
      <comment>/* Radiant heat in the home */ ...</comment>
      <text xml:space="preserve">'''Thermal radiation''', or '''radiant heat''', is [[electromagnetic radiation]] from an object that is simply caused by its [[temperature]]. It rapidly increases in [[power]], and in [[frequency]], with increasing temperature.

==Examples of thermal radiation==
Radiant heat has numerous applications everywhere from the [[home]] to [[outer space]].

===Radiant heat in the home===

When a home radiant heat system is turned on, electricity is forced through a [[conductive]] heating material. For high [[voltage]] radiant heat systems, line voltage (110 V) of electricity is forced through the heating cable. For low voltage systems, the line voltage is converted to low voltage (8-30 V.) of electricity in the control unit (which contains a step-down [[transformer]]) and this low [[current]] is then forced through the heating element. 

The heated material then heats the flooring until it reaches the right temperature set by the floor thermostat. The radiant heat then passes through the floor and continues to heat other objects in the room (tables, chairs, people). The heat will continue to heat the room and its objects up to the ceiling. Radiant heat will give you the most consistent room temperature from floor to ceiling compared to any other heating system.

===Radiant heat in outer space===

A [[black body]] is an object that absorbs all electromagnetic radiation that falls onto it. For this case there are formulas for the power as a function of temperature, etc.

A device specially made for thermal radiation may have different purposes:
*the radiation itself may be the objective, as in a [[light bulb]]
*the objective may be local cooling

For example, spacecraft may have '''thermal radiators''', also called '''heat radiators''', to lose excess heat &amp;mdash; the [[Space Shuttle]] has heat radiators mounted on the inner surfaces of the payload bay doors, and these are kept open while the Shuttle is in [[orbit]] to allow the radiators to function.  Spacecraft radiators tend to be reflective in the visible spectrum, to avoid absorption of solar radiation energy, while good emitters in the infra-red so that they can radiate heat easily.  Special [[paints]] and coatings are used, designed to have this property and maintain it under space conditions.  Ironically, although they are used as ''black body'' radiators, these coatings appear white to the eye, since they need to reflect visible wavelengths to avoid absorbing them.

Outer space is an effective black body with respect to the [[earth]]. If it's a clear night (i.e., no clouds are present), the surface of the earth radiates heat to space. This radiation of heat from earth to space is noticeable with regard to the formation of [[dew]] or [[frost]]. On cloudy nights the clouds shield the radiation of heat into space.

Radiating heat to the cold cosmos has also been used to form ice in well-insulated shallow bowls even if the air temperature is above freezing.

Thermal radiation normally lies in the frequency range of λ = 0.8 to 400 μm, within that spectrum visible light is at λ = 0.35 to 0.75 μm

== Equation ==
Thermal radiation, in [[watt]]s, is equal to:
:&lt;math&gt;W = \sigma A k_B T^4&lt;/math&gt;
where
:''&lt;math&gt;\sigma&lt;/math&gt;'' = [[Emissivity]]
:''&lt;math&gt;k_B&lt;/math&gt;'' = [[Stefan-Boltzmann constant]]
:''A'' = Surface area
:''T'' = Temperature in [[kelvin]]s

== See also==
* [[Heat conduction]]
* [[Convection]]

== External links ==
*[http://sol.sci.uop.edu/~jfalward/heattransfer/heattransfer.html http://sol.sci.uop.edu/~jfalward/heattransfer/heattransfer.html] - website on heat transfer
*[http://www.warmzone.com/glossary.asp Online Glossary of Radiant Terms can be found here]

[[Category:Electromagnetic radiation]]
[[Category:Thermodynamics]]

[[de:Wärmestrahlung]]
[[nl:Warmtestraling]]
[[pt:Radiação térmica]]
[[it:Irraggiamento termico]]</text>
    </revision>
