  <page>
    <title>Agranulocytosis</title>
    <id>185373</id>
    <revision>
      <id>40952074</id>
      <timestamp>2006-02-24T02:07:30Z</timestamp>
      <contributor>
        <username>Rmky87</username>
        <id>325543</id>
      </contributor>
      <minor />
      <comment>+de</comment>
      <text xml:space="preserve">{{DiseaseDisorder infobox |
  Name        = Agranulocytosis |
  ICD10       = {{ICD10|D|70||d|70}} |
  ICD9        = {{ICD9|288.0}} |
}}
{{mergeto|Neutropenia}}
'''Agranulocytosis''' (literally means a lack of [[granulocyte]]s) is an acute condition involving a severe and dangerous [[leukopenia]] (reduction in the number of [[white blood cell|white]] [[blood cell]]s) in the body. Concentrations of granulocytes (a class which include [[neutrophils]], [[basophils]] and [[eosinophils]]) can often drop to below 500 cells/mm&lt;sup&gt;3&lt;/sup&gt; of blood), less than a sixth of the reference value of 3-10 x 10&lt;sup&gt;3&lt;/sup&gt; cells/mm&lt;sup&gt;3&lt;/sup&gt;. 

A large number of drugs have been associated with agranulocytosis, including [[antiepileptic]]s, [[antithyroid drug]]s and some [[antipsychotic]]s (the atypical antipsychotic [[clozapine]]). Although the reaction is generally idiosyncratic rather than proportional, experts recommend that patients be told about the symptoms of agranulocytosis (generally starting with a sore throat and a fever).

The terms &quot;agranulocytosis&quot;, [[granulocytopenia]], and [[neutropenia]] are often used interchangably, although &quot;agranulocytosis&quot; implies a more severe deficiency than &quot;granulocytopenia&quot;, and &quot;neutropenia&quot; implies a deficiency of [[neutrophil]]s only. The following terms can be used to specify the type of [[granulocyte]] referenced:

* [[neutrophil]] -- [[neutropenia]]
* [[eosinophil]] -- [[eosinopenia]] - not commonly used
* [[basophil]] -- [[basopenia]] - very rarely used

==See also==
* [[Complete blood count]]
* [[Granulocytosis]]

{{med-stub}}

[[Category:Blood disorders]]
[[de:Agranulozytose]]
[[fr:Agranulocytose]]</text>
    </revision>
  </page>
  <page>
    <title>Grammy Awards of 1978</title>
    <id>185375</id>
    <revision>
      <id>40962162</id>
      <timestamp>2006-02-24T03:34:02Z</timestamp>
      <contributor>
        <username>Rbraunwa</username>
        <id>13136</id>
      </contributor>
      <comment>Added presentation date and an external link</comment>
      <text xml:space="preserve">The 20th [[Grammy]] Awards were held [[February 23]], [[1978]], and were broadcast live on American television. They recognized accomplishments by musicians from the year [[1977]].

==Award winners:==

*[[Grammy Award for Record of the Year|Record of the Year]]
**[[Bill Szymczyk]] (producer) &amp; [[The Eagles]] for &quot;Hotel California&quot;

*[[Grammy Award for Album of the Year|Album of the Year]]
**[[Ken Caillat]], [[Richard Dashut]] (producers) &amp; [[Fleetwood Mac]] (producers and artist) for ''Rumours''

*[[Grammy Award for Song of the Year|Song of the Year]]
**[[Barbra Streisand]] &amp; [[Paul Williams (songwriter)|Paul Williams]] (songwriters) for &quot;Love Theme From ''A Star Is Born'' (Evergreen)&quot; performed by Barbra Streisand
**[[Joe Brooks]] (songwriter) for &quot;You Light Up My Life&quot; performed by [[Debby Boone]]

*[[Grammy Award for Best New Artist|Best New Artist]]
**[[Debby Boone]]

===Children's===

*[[Grammy Award for Best Album for Children|Best Recording for Children]]
**[[Christopher Cerf]] &amp; [[Jim Timmens]] (producers) for ''Aren't You Glad You're You'' performed by various artists

===Classical===

*[[Grammy Award for Best Orchestral Performance|Best Classical Orchestral Performance]]
**[[Gunther Breest]] (producer), [[Carlo Maria Giulini]] (conductor) &amp; the [[Chicago Symphony Orchestra]] for ''[[Gustav Mahler|Mahler]]: Symphony No. 9 in D''
*[[Grammy Award for Best Classical Vocal Performance|Best Classical Vocal Soloist Performance]]
**[[Neville Marriner]] (conductor), [[Janet Baker]] &amp; the [[Academy of St. Martin in the Fields]] for ''[[Johann Sebastian Bach|Bach]]: Arias''
*[[Grammy Award for Best Opera Recording|Best Opera Recording]]
**[[Thomas Z. Shepard]] (producer), [[John De Main]] (conductor), [[Donnie Albert]], [[Carol Brice]], [[Clamma Dale]] &amp; the [[Houston Grand Opera Orchestra]] for ''[[George Gershwin|Gershwin]]: [[Porgy and Bess]]''
*[[Grammy Award for Best Choral Performance|Best Choral Performance (other than opera)]]
**[[Georg Solti]] (conductor), [[Margaret Hillis]] (choir director) &amp; the [[Chicago Symphony Orchestra|Chicago Symphony Orchestra &amp; Chorus]] for ''[[Giuseppe Verdi|Verdi]]: Requiem''
*[[Grammy Award for Best Instrumental Soloist(s) Performance (with orchestra)|Best Classical Performance Instrumental Soloist or Soloists (with orchestra)]]
**[[Itzhak Perlman]] &amp; the [[London Philharmonic Orchestra]] for ''[[Antonio Vivaldi|Vivaldi]]: The Four Seasons''      
*[[Grammy Award for Best Instrumental Soloist Performance (without orchestra)|Best Classical Performance Instrumental Soloist or Soloists (without orchestra)]]
**[[Arthur Rubinstein]] for ''[[Ludwig van Beethoven|Beethoven]]: Piano Sonata No. 18 in E Flat/[[Robert Schumann|Schumann]]: Fantasiestucke, Op. 12''
*[[Grammy Award for Best Chamber Music Performance|Best Chamber Music Performance]]
**The [[Juilliard String Quartet]] for ''[[Arnold Schoenberg|Schoenberg]]: Quartets for Strings (Complete)''      
*[[Grammy Award for Best Classical Album|Best Classical Album]]
**[[Thomas Frost]] (producer), [[Leonard Bernstein]] (conductor), [[Dietrich Fischer-Dieskau]], [[Vladimir Horowitz]], [[Yehudi Menuhin]], [[Mstislav Rostropovich]], [[Isaac Stern]], [[Lyndon Woodside]] &amp; the [[New York Philharmonic]] for ''Concert of the Century''

===Comedy===

*[[Grammy Award for Best Comedy Album|Best Comedy Recording]]
**[[Steve Martin]] for ''Let's Get Small''

===Composing and arranging===

*[[Grammy Award for Best Instrumental Composition|Best Instrumental Composition]]
**[[John Williams]] (composer) for &quot;Main Title From ''Star Wars''&quot;
*[[Grammy Award for Best Score Soundtrack Album for a Motion Picture, Television or Other Visual Media|Best Original Score Written for a Motion Picture or a Television Special]]
**John Williams (composer) for ''Star Wars'' 
*[[Grammy Award for Best Instrumental Arrangement|Best Instrumental Arrangement]]
**[[Harry Betts]], [[Perry Botkin Jr.]] &amp; [[Barry De Vorzon]] (arrangers) for &quot;[[Nadia's Theme]] (''The Young and the Restless'')&quot; performed by Barry De Vorzon
*[[Grammy Award for Best Instrumental Arrangement Accompanying Vocalist(s)|Best Arrangement Accompanying Vocalist(s)]]
**[[Ian Freebairn-Smith]] (arranger) for &quot;Love Theme From ''A Star Is Born'' (Evergreen)&quot; performed by [[Barbra Streisand]]
*[[Grammy Award for Best Vocal Arrangement for Two or More Voices|Best Arrangement For Voices]]
**[[The Eagles]] (arrangers) for &quot;New Kid in Town&quot;

===Country===

*[[Grammy Award for Best Female Country Vocal Performance|Best Country Vocal Performance, Female]]
**[[Crystal Gayle]] for &quot;Don't It Make My Brown Eyes Blue&quot;
*[[Grammy Award for Best Male Country Vocal Performance|Best Country Vocal Performance, Male]]
**[[Kenny Rogers]] for &quot;Lucille&quot;
*[[Grammy Award for Best Country Performance by a Duo or Group with Vocal|Best Country Vocal Performance by a Duo or Group]]
**[[The Kendalls]] for &quot;Heaven's Just a Sin Away&quot;
*[[Grammy Award for Best Country Instrumental Performance|Best Country Instrumental Performance]]
**[[Hargus Pig Robbins]] for ''Country Instrumentalist of the Year''
*[[Grammy Award for Best Country Song|Best Country Song]]
**[[Richard Leigh]] (songwriter) for &quot;Don't It Make My Brown Eyes Blue&quot; performed by [[Crystal Gayle]]

===Folk===

*[[Grammy Award for Best Ethnic or Traditional Folk Recording|Best Ethnic or Traditional Recording]]
**[[Muddy Waters]] for ''Hard Again''

===Gospel===

*[[Grammy Award for Best Gospel Performance, Traditional|Best Gospel Performance, Traditional]] 
**[[The Oak Ridge Boys]] for &quot;Just a Little Talk With Jesus&quot;
*[[Grammy Award for Best Gospel Performance, Contemporary|Best Gospel Performance, Contemporary or Insprirational]] 
**[[The Imperials]] for ''Sail On''
*[[Grammy Award for Best Soul Gospel Performance, Traditional|Best Soul Gospel Performance, Traditional]]
**[[James Cleveland]] for ''James Cleveland Live at Carnegie Hall''
*[[Grammy Award for Best Soul Gospel Performance, Contemporary|Best Soul Gospel Performance, Contemporary]]
**[[Edwin Hawkins]] for ''Wonderful!''
*[[Grammy Award for Best Inspirational Performance|Best Inspirational Performance]]
**[[B. J. Thomas]] for ''Home Where I Belong''

===Jazz===

*[[Grammy Award for Best Jazz Instrumental Solo|Best Jazz Performance by a Soloist  ]]
**[[Oscar Peterson]] for ''The Giants''
*[[Grammy Award for Best Jazz Instrumental Album, Individual or Group|Best Jazz Performance by a Group]]
**[[Phil Woods]] for ''The Phil Woods Six - Live From the Showboat''
*[[Grammy Award for Best Large Jazz Ensemble Album|Best Jazz Performance by a Big Band]]
**[[Count Basie]] for ''Prime Time''
*[[Grammy Award for Best Jazz Vocal Album|Best Jazz Vocal Performance]]
**[[Al Jarreau]] for ''Look to the Rainbow''

===Latin===

*[[Grammy Award for Best Latin Recording|Best Latin Recording]]
**[[Mongo Santamaria]] for ''Dawn''

===Musical Show===

*[[Grammy Award for Best Musical Show Album|Best Cast Show Album]]
** [[Martin Charnin]] (composer), [[Charles Strouse]] (composer &amp; producer), [[Larry Morton]] (producer) &amp; the original cast with [[Andrea McCardle]] &amp; [[Dorothy Loudon]] for ''Annie''

===Packaging and Notes===

*[[Grammy Award for Best Recording Package|Best Album Package]]
**[[John Kosh]] (art director) for ''Simple Dreams'' performed by [[Linda Ronstadt]]
*[[Grammy Award for Best Album Notes|Best Album Notes]]
**[[George T. Simon]] (notes writer) for ''Bing Crosby - A Legendary Performer'' performed by Bing Crosby

===Pop===

*[[Grammy Award for Best Female Pop Vocal Performance|Best Pop Vocal Performance, Female]]
**[[Barbra Streisand]] for &quot;Love Theme From ''A Star Is Born''&quot;
*[[Grammy Award for Best Male Pop Vocal Performance|Best Pop Vocal Performance, Male]]
**[[James Taylor]] for &quot;Handy Man&quot;
*[[Grammy Award for Best Pop Performance by a Duo or Group with Vocal|Best Pop Performance by a Group]]
**[[Bee Gees]] for &quot;How Deep Is Your Love&quot;
*[[Grammy Award for Best Pop Instrumental Performance|Best Pop Instrumental Performance]]
**John Williams for ''[[Star Wars Episode IV: A New Hope|Star Wars]]''

===Production and engineering===

*[[Grammy Award for Best Engineered Album, Non-Classical|Best Engineered Recording, Non-Classical]]
**[[Al Schmitt]], [[Bill Schnee]], [[Elliot Scheiner]] &amp; [[Roger Nichols]] (engineers) for ''Aja'' performed by [[Steely Dan]]
*[[Grammy Award for Best Engineered Album, Classical|Best Engineered Recording, Classical]]
**[[Kenneth Wilkinson]] (engineer), [[Georg Solti]] (conductor) &amp; the [[Chicago Symphony Orchestra]] for ''[[Maurice RavelRavel]]: Bolero''
*[[Grammy Award for Producer of the Year, Non-Classical|Producer of the Year]]
**[[Peter Asher]]

===R&amp;B===

*[[Grammy Award for Best Female R&amp;B Vocal Performance|Best R&amp;B Vocal Performance, Female]]
**[[Thelma Houston]] for &quot;Don't Leave Me This Way&quot;
*[[Grammy Award for Best Male R&amp;B Vocal Performance|Best R&amp;B Vocal Performance, Male]]
**[[Lou Rawls]] for ''Unmistakably Lou''
*[[Grammy Award for Best R&amp;B Performance by a Duo or Group with Vocals|Best R&amp;B Vocal Performance by a Duo, Group or Chorus]]
**[[The Emotions]] for &quot;Best of My Love&quot;
*[[Grammy Award for Best R&amp;B Instrumental Performance|Best R&amp;B Instrumental Performance]]
**[[Brothers Johnson]] for &quot;Q&quot;
*[[Grammy Award for Best R&amp;B Song|Best Rhythm &amp; Blues Song]]
**[[Leo Sayer]] &amp; [[Vini Poncia]] (songwriters) for &quot;You Make Me Feel Like Dancing&quot; performed by Leo Sayer

===Spoken===

*[[Grammy Award for Best Spoken Word Album|Best Spoken Word Recording]]
**[[Julie Harris]] for ''The Belle of Amherst''

==External links==
*[http://imdb.com/title/tt0341206/ 20th Grammy Awards] at the Internet Movie Database


{{Grammy Award years}}
