        <username>Rbraunwa</username>
        <id>13136</id>
      </contributor>
      <comment>Added the date of the awards and an external link</comment>
      <text xml:space="preserve">The 18th [[Grammy]] Awards were held [[February 28]], [[1976]], and were broadcast live on American television. They recognized accomplishments by musicians from the year [[1975]].

==Award winners:==

*[[Grammy Award for Record of the Year|Record of the Year]]
**[[Daryl Dragon]] (producer) &amp; [[Captain &amp; Tennille]] for &quot;Love Will Keep Us Together&quot;

*[[Grammy Award for Album of the Year|Album of the Year]]
**[[Phil Ramone]] (producer) &amp; [[Paul Simon]] (producer &amp; artist) for ''Still Crazy After All These Years''

*[[Grammy Award for Song of the Year|Song of the Year]]
**[[Stephen Sondheim]] (songwriter) for &quot;Send In the Clowns&quot; performed by [[Judy Collins]]

*[[Grammy Award for Best New Artist|Best New Artist]]
**[[Natalie Cole]]

===Children's===

*[[Grammy Award for Best Album for Children|Best Recording for Children]]
**[[Richard Burton]] for ''The Little Prince''

===Classical=== 

*[[Grammy Award for Best Orchestral Performance|Best Classical Performance - Orchestra]]
**[[Pierre Boulez]] (conductor), the [[Camarata Singers]] &amp; the [[New York Philharmonic]] for ''[[Maurice Ravel|Ravel]]: [[Daphnis et Chloé]] (Complete Ballet)''
*[[Grammy Award for Best Classical Vocal Performance|Best Classical Vocal Performance]] 
**[[Janet Baker]] for ''[[Gustav Mahler|Mahler]]: Kindertotenlieder''
*[[Grammy Award for Best Opera Recording|Best Opera Recording]]
**[[Erik Smith]] (producer), [[Colin Davis]] (conductor),  [[Richard van Allan]], [[Janet Baker]], [[Montserrat Caballe]], [[Ileana Contrubas]], [[Vladimiro Ganzarolli]], [[Nicolai Gedda]] &amp; the [[Royal Opera House Orchestra]] for ''[[Wolfgang Amadeus Mozart|Mozart]]: Cosi Fan Tutte''
*[[Grammy Award for Best Choral Performance|Best Choral Performance, Classical (other than opera)]]
**[[Michael Tilson Thomas]] (conductor), [[Robert Page]] (choir director) the [[Cleveland Boys Choir]] &amp; [[Cleveland Orchestra|Cleveland Orchestra Chorus]] for ''[[Carl Orff|Orff]]: Carmina Burana''
*[[Grammy Award for Best Instrumental Soloist(s) Performance (with orchestra)|Best Classical Performance Instrumental Soloist or Soloists (with orchestra)]]
**[[Rafael Fruhbeck de Burgos]] (conductor), [[Alicia de Larrocha]] &amp; the [[London Philharmonic]] for ''[[Maurice Ravel|Ravel]]: Concerto for Left Hand and Concerto for Piano in G/[[Gabriel Fauré|Fauré]]: Fantaisie for Piano and Orchestra'' 
*[[Grammy Award for Best Instrumental Soloist Performance (without orchestra)|Best Classical Performance Instrumental Soloist or Soloists (without orchestra)]] 
**[[Nathan Milstein]] for ''[[Johann Sebastian Bach|Bach]]: [[Sonatas and partitas for solo violin (1001-1006)|Sonatas and Partitas for Violin Unaccompanied]]''
*[[Grammy Award for Best Chamber Music Performance|Best Chamber Music Performance]]
**[[Pierre Fournier]], [[Arthur Rubinstein]] &amp; [[Henryk Szeryng]] for ''[[Franz Schubert|Schubert]]: Trios Nos. 1 in B Flat, Op. 99 and 2 in E Flat, Op. 100 (Piano Trios)''
*[[Grammy Award for Best Classical Album|Album of the Year, Classical]] 
**[[Raymond Minshull]] (producer), [[Georg Solti]] (conductor) &amp; the [[Chicago Symphony Orchestra]] for ''[[Ludwig van Beethoven|Beethoven]]: Symphonies (9) Complete''

===Comedy===

*[[Grammy Award for Best Comedy Album|Best Comedy Recording]] 
**[[Richard Pryor]] for ''Is It Something I Said?''

===Composing and arranging===

*[[Grammy Award for Best Instrumental Composition|Best Instrumental Composition]] 
**[[Michel Legrand]] (composer) for ''Images'' performed by Michel Legrand &amp; [[Phil Woods]]
*[[Grammy Award for Best Score Soundtrack Album for a Motion Picture, Television or Other Visual Media|Album of Best Original Score Written for a Motion Picture or a Television Special]]
**[[John Williams]] (composer) for ''Jaws''
*[[Grammy Award for Best Instrumental Arrangement|Best Instrumental Arrangement]]
**[[Pete Clarence Carpenter]] &amp; [[Mike Post]] (arrangers) for &quot;The Rockford Files&quot; performed by Mike Post
*[[Grammy Award for Best Instrumental Arrangement Accompanying Vocalist(s)|Best Arrangement Accompanying Vocalist(s)]]
**[[Ray Stevens]] (arranger) for &quot;Misty&quot;

===Country===

*[[Grammy Award for Best Female Country Vocal Performance|Best Country Vocal Performance, Female]] 
**[[Linda Ronstadt]] for &quot;I Can't Help It (If I'm Still in Love With You)&quot;
*[[Grammy Award for Best Male Country Vocal Performance|Best Country Vocal Performance, Male]]
**[[Willie Nelson]] for &quot;Blue Eyes Crying in the Rain&quot;
*[[Grammy Award for Best Country Performance by a Duo or Group with Vocal|Best Country Vocal Performance by a Duo or Group]]
**[[Rita Coolidge]] &amp; [[Kris Kristofferson]] for &quot;Lover Please&quot;
*[[Grammy Award for Best Country Instrumental Performance|Best Country Instrumental Performance]]
**[[Chet Atkins]] for &quot;The Entertainer&quot;
*[[Grammy Award for Best Country Song|Best Country Song]] 
**[[Larry Butler]] &amp; [[Chips Moman]] (songwriters) for &quot;(Hey Won't You Play) Another Somebody Done Somebody Wrong Song&quot; performed by [[B. J. Thomas]]

===Folk=== 

*[[Grammy Award for Best Ethnic or Traditional Folk Recording|Best Ethnic or Traditional Recording]]
**[[Muddy Waters]] for ''The Muddy Waters Woodstock Album''

===Gospel===

*[[Grammy Award for Best Gospel Performance|Best Gospel Performance (other than soul gospel)]] 
**[[The Imperials]] for ''No Shortage''
*[[Grammy Award for Best Soul Gospel Performance|Best Soul Gospel Performance]]
**[[Andrae Crouch]] for ''Take Me Back'' performed by [[Andrae Crouch &amp; the Disciples]]
*[[Grammy Award for Best Inspirational Performance|Best Inspirational Performance]]
**The [[Bill Gaither Trio]] for ''Jesus, We Just Want to Thank You''

===Jazz=== 

*[[Grammy Award for Best Jazz Instrumental Solo|Best Jazz Performance by a Soloist]]
**[[Dizzy Gillespie]] for ''Oscar Peterson and Dizzy Gillespie''
*[[Grammy Award for Best Jazz Instrumental Album, Individual or Group|Best Jazz Performance by a Group]]
**[[Chick Corea]] &amp; [[Return to Forever]] for ''No Mystery''
*[[Grammy Award for Best Large Jazz Ensemble Album|Best Jazz Performance by a Big Band]]
**[[Michel Legrand]] &amp; [[Phil Woods]] for ''Images''

===Latin===

*[[Grammy Award for Best Latin Recording|Best Latin Recording]]
**[[Eddie Palmieri]] for ''Sun of Latin Music''

===Musical Show===

*[[Grammy Award for Best Musical Show Album|Best Cast Show Album]]
**[[Charlie Smalls]] (composer),  [[Jerry Wexler]] (producer) &amp; the original cast with [[Stephanie Mills]] &amp; [[Dee Dee Bridgewater]] for ''The Wiz''

===Packaging and Notes===

*[[Grammy Award for Best Recording Package|Best Album Package]]
**[[Jim Ladwig]] (art director) for ''Honey'' performed by the [[Ohio Players]]
*[[Grammy Award for Best Album Notes|Best Album Notes]]
**[[Pete Hamill]] (notes writer) for ''Blood on the Tracks'' performed by [[Bob Dylan]]
*[[Grammy Award for Best Album Notes - Classical|Best Album Notes - Classical]]
**[[Gunther Schuller]] (notes writer) for ''Footlifters'' performed by Gunther Schuller

===Pop=== 

*[[Grammy Award for Best Female Pop Vocal Performance|Best Pop Vocal Performance, Female]] 
**[[Janis Ian]] for &quot;At Seventeen&quot;
*[[Grammy Award for Best Male Pop Vocal Performance|Best Pop Vocal Performance, Male]]
**[[Paul Simon]] for ''Still Crazy After All These Years''
*[[Grammy Award for Best Pop Performance by a Duo or Group with Vocal|Best Pop Vocal Performance by a Duo, Group or Chorus]]
**[[The Eagles]] for &quot;Lyin' Eyes&quot;
*[[Grammy Award for Best Pop Instrumental Performance|Best Pop Instrumental Performance]]
**[[Van McCoy]] for &quot;The Hustle&quot;

===Production and engineering===

*[[Grammy Award for Best Engineered Album, Non-Classical|Best Engineered Recording, Non-Classical]]
**[[Brooks Arthur]], [[Larry Alexander]] &amp; [[Russ Payne]] (engineers) for ''Between the Lines'' performed by [[Janis Ian]]
*[[Grammy Award for Best Engineered Album, Classical|Best Engineered Recording, Classical]]
**[[Edward (Bud) T. Graham]], [[Milton Cherin]], [[Ray Moore]] (engineers), [[Pierre Boulez]] (conductor), the [[Camarata Singers]] &amp; the [[New York Philharmonic]] for ''[[Maurice Ravel|Ravel]]: Daphnis et Chloe (Complete Ballet)''
*[[Grammy Award for Producer of the Year, Non-Classical|Producer of the Year, Non-Classical]]
**[[Arif Mardin]]

===R&amp;B=== 

*[[Grammy Award for Best Female R&amp;B Vocal Performance|Best R&amp;B Vocal Performance, Female]]
**[[Natalie Cole]] for &quot;This Will Be&quot;      
*[[Grammy Award for Best Male R&amp;B Vocal Performance|Best R&amp;B Vocal Performance, Male]]
**[[Ray Charles]] for &quot;Living for the City&quot;      
*[[Grammy Award for Best R&amp;B Performance by a Duo or Group with Vocals|Best R&amp;B Vocal Performance by a Duo, Group or Chorus]]
**[[Earth, Wind &amp; Fire]] for &quot;Shining Star&quot;
*[[Grammy Award for Best R&amp;B Instrumental Performance|Best R&amp;B Instrumental Performance]]
**[[Silver Convention]] for &quot;Fly, Robin, Fly&quot;
*[[Grammy Award for Best R&amp;B Song|Best Rhythm &amp; Blues Song]]
**[[Harry Wayne Casey]], [[Willie Clarke]], [[Richard Finch]] &amp; [[Betty Wright]] (songwriters) for &quot;Where Is the Love&quot; performed by [[Roberta Flack]] &amp; [[Donny Hathaway]]

===Spoken=== 

*[[Grammy Award for Best Spoken Word Album|Best Spoken Word Recording]] 
**[[James Whitmore]] for ''Give 'Em Hell Harry''

==External links==
*[http://www.imdb.com/Sections/Awards/Grammy_Awards/1976 The 18th Grammay Awards], at the Internet Movie Database


